5:He catalogued the camp as he sat up, because it was the first morning he had ever woken beside a line. The stone in its rut, two fists of it, dew on the top and none underneath. The three boxes on the Thornwater side of the stone, one, two, three, as Brom had set them down. The mule, hipshot, cropping the verge with its rope round a scrub root. Seln, sitting where he had sat down the evening before, facing the way they had come, who might have slept and might not. Lira with the ankle's tape already done. Oryn on her back on the road with her coat over her and her hands folded on the coat, awake, looking at the sky. And Karis at the stone with the cord in both hands, running it through her fingers knot by knot, as a woman counts money she has counted already.
29:Karis did not stop. She had her own sigil for the other edge, and Cael watched it work on her face: a small check every few strides, the eyes going to the place and coming away, the way a man crossing a river checks the far bank. Twice in the first ten cords she said "Gone," quietly, of herself, and moved a half-stride out, and said "Back," and went on. Between Lira's stride and hers the line lay somewhere, and it never once lay wider than the two of them.
105:Karis went in without being asked, and stood a stride inside with the book under her arm and her scarred right hand out in front of her, and Cael saw her see it: the whole lattice, the way she saw it before she lit one, the points laid out on the air in the order her hand would take them.
125:They stepped over the stone together, her hands on his arm, and Cael watched her face because there was nothing else to watch, and saw the count stop in it. Not end. Stop, the way a man's breath stops at a tape.
193:The floor. He walked it edge to edge, twenty-six strides along the road's line, and twenty-six across it, and it was round to the eye. Level to the eye; and Karis ran her cord taut from one upright's foot to another's across the middle and lay down on the stone with her cheek on it and sighted along the cord, and moved the cord, and sighted again, and there was no daylight under it anywhere. Seamless. He went down on his knees at the middle, where the breath stopped at the tape, and put both palms flat, and let the read run on the stone under them, privately, a man touching a floor: it gave him one piece of stone from his hands to the lip, no join, no seam, no laid edge, nothing under the surface but more of the surface. Fused. Not laid. He did not write that word. He wrote *no seam* and let the read say the rest to him alone. At the edge, all the way round, the floor rose the height of a hand and turned over, as the rim of a shallow basin turns, so that a man standing on the grass outside it stood at a lip.
213:He had walked round the floor's edge on the outside of the lip while the rest were on the floor, slowly, looking at the ground the way he looked at a road, and at the far side, where the grass came up to the lip and the lip stood a forearm proud of it, he went down and stayed down, and did not call. Cael saw him stay down and went; and then Brom; and then the rest.
